Ladybird ends a run stopped by SIGINT or SIGTERM the way a quit ends it:
When the session store saves the session, a handler on the event loop
marks the store quitting and stops the loop, without asking any page
first — so the teardown that follows records a clean exit.

Chromium handles SIGHUP, SIGINT, and SIGTERM much the same, through its
ShutdownDetector: ExitHandler::Exit() calls chrome::AttemptExit(), after
calling chrome::ExitIgnoreUnloadHandlers() on ChromeOS. And its
GracefulShutdownHandler() puts the default handler back at once (“We had
one shot at graceful shutdown”), so a second signal ends even a browser
whose UI thread is hung. Ladybird’s handlers run on the event loop
instead, so a hung loop ignores every signal but SIGKILL.

Firefox handles only SIGTERM, and only in its GTK builds, where
nsAppShell::TermSignalHandler() schedules a quit event that exits the
app shell’s main event loop.
